  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a dry start to the day with some patchy fog, scattered \nshowers and isolated T-storms are expected to develop this \nafternoon, as a cold front approaches from the Great Lakes and \nSE Canada. Guidance continues to show this front weakening as it\nenters our area, so will only mention shower chances mainly for\nareas north/west of Albany. A few showers may get close to the \nCapital District towards evening as the front moves in, so will \nmention 20% PoPs. Guidance still showing limited instability \nwith SBCAPE < 700 J/Kg from HREF, so will continue to only \nmention slight chance of thunder. \n\nThe decaying front shifts south of the area tonight into Thu, \nalthough much of the latest guidance has the remnants of the \nboundary only reaching SE PA/NJ before stalling with a wave of \nlow pressure developing along the old boundary and tracking east\nalong it on Thu. CAMs are especially aggressive indicating \nshowers from this wave making it far enough north into at least\nsouthern parts of our area (E. Catskills, mid Hudson Valley, \nTaconics, Litchfield CT). Global guidance is not as aggressive \nthough, but AI models do have showers south of Albany. NBM \nPoPs/QPF look too low, so have increased PoPs to likely for \nthese areas by Thu P.M. and used WPC QPF, which shows around \n0.25-0.50\" across the far southern part of the area. The threat \nfor any severe storms remains very low, as any appreciable \ninstability should be displaced well south of our region. As a \nresult of more clouds/showers around, temperatures look cooler \n(mainly 70s) and may need to be lowered further if trends hold. \nThe showers should taper off from west to east Thu evening as \nthe wave tracks into SE New England.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe pattern looks to become more active by the later half of \nthe weekend into early next week, as a deep upper level trough \nsettles in across the Great Lakes and Northeast. The weekend is \ncoming into somewhat better focus now. Sat looks like the drier \nday compared to Sun, although will mention chance PoPs \nsouth/east of Albany due to potential for some showers along the\nnorthern periphery of a coastal wave expected to track from the\nmid Atlantic coast to SE New England. The more widespread \nshowers should be south of our area based on the current \nguidance. Sun is expected to be the wetter day, as forcing \nincreases downstream of the upper trough axis with diffluence \naloft. With the main surface cyclone expected to track north \ninto Canada, the system's occluded front should provide low \nlevel focus for likely rounds of showers (and perhaps some \nT-storms) to move across our area. The threat of heavy rain has \ndecreased, as most deterministic, AI and ensemble guidance have \nloosely consolidated indicating a closed low tracking north/west\nof our area. There is still spread in the exact track, but \nlatest trends at least indicate lowering potential for heavy \nrainfall. Latest NBM showing a 15-35% prob of > 1.0\" rain ending\nMonday morning. Temperatures look to be below normal through \nearly next week associated with the trough.",
